<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Marhashi</h1><p class="st-meta">2500 BCE – 1900 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A wealthy kingdom of the Iranian plateau, east of Elam, that traded luxury stone vessels and exotic animals with Mesopotamia and fought the kings of Akkad.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Marhashi (Parahshum in Akkadian) lay beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, probably in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiroft">Jiroft</a> region of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> Jiroft culture produced carved chlorite vessels found as far afield as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. It is first mentioned in the mid-third millennium BCE as a powerful eastern land.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Mesopotamian texts present Marhashi as a large kingdom with its own kings and wide trade links across the plateau to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>, supplying semi-precious stones, chlorite bowls and animals such as the 'dogs of Marhashi'. Its kings allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimush">Rimush</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>, and Rimush records defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abalgamash">Abalgamash</a>, king of Marhashi. Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a> relations were peaceful: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulgi">Shulgi</a> married a daughter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libanukshabash">Libanukshabash</a> of Marhashi.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Marhashi is last mentioned in the early second millennium BCE, and its decline mirrors the collapse of the trading towns of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> around 1900 BCE. Its identification with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiroft">Jiroft</a> finds remains a hypothesis.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/marhashi.jpg" alt="Marhashi" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur; derivative work by Zunkir · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moyen_Orient_3mil_aC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2500 BCE – 1900 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/26th-century-bce">26th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century-bce">20th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abalgamash, Libanukshabash</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Parahshum, Barahshum</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-2200&amp;focus=marhashi" aria-label="Marhashi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6100 992 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Marhashi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6129 1232h0M6193 1305h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6420 1248h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6420 1248h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iran. <a href="/atlas?year=-2200&amp;focus=marhashi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

